{"name":"Little Blue Heron","hex":"#3c4378","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"little blue heron","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Egretta caerulea, a small dark heron with a two-toned bill."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/little_blue_heron"}],"entries":[{"title":"Little blue heron","blocks":[{"text":"The little blue heron is a small heron of the genus Egretta. It is a small, darkly colored heron with a two-toned bill. Juveniles are entirely white, bearing resemblance to the snowy egret. During the breeding season, adults develop different coloration on the head, legs, and feet."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/Little_Blue_Fly-By.png/330px-Little_Blue_Fly-By.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_blue_heron","wikidata":"Q371028"},{"title":"Heron","blocks":[{"text":"Herons are long-legged, long-necked, freshwater and coastal birds in the family Ardeidae, with 75 recognised species, some of which are referred to as egrets or bitterns rather than herons. Members of the genus Botaurus are referred to as bitterns, and, together with the zigzag heron, or zigzag bittern, in the monotypic genus Zebrilus, form a monophyletic group, Botaurinae, within the Ardeidae family. Egrets do not form a biologically distinct group from herons, and tend to be named differently because they are mainly white or have decorative plumes in breeding plumage. Herons, by evolutionary adaptation, have long beaks."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ardeidae-01.jpg/330px-Ardeidae-01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heron","wikidata":"Q18789"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg/500px-Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg/1280px-Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg/500px-Egretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Little blue heron in Cananeia, Sao Paulo, Brazil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEgretta_caerulea_-Cananeia%2C_Sao_Paulo%2C_Brasil-8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg/500px-Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg/1280px-Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg/500px-Little_blue_heron_%28Egretta_caerulea%29_juvenile_Cayo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"juvenile, Macal River, Cayo, Belize","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALittle_blue_heron_(Egretta_caerulea)_juvenile_Cayo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg/500px-Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg/1280px-Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg/500px-Little_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" A close up of a little blue heron's head at Lake Apopka, Florida","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALittle_Blue_Heron_Portrait.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Goliath_heron_standing_cropped.jpg/500px-Goliath_heron_standing_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Goliath_heron_standing_cropped.jpg/500px-Goliath_heron_standing_cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The goliath heron is the largest heron species.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoliath_heron_standing_cropped.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Yellow_Bitter